// Generated GridLAB-D model from the CINELDI LV reference grid
// Source: ../../../cineldi_lv_reference_system/lv_reference_grids/56_bus_semi_urban_reference_grid
// Regenerated by scripts/regen_reference_glm.py -- do not hand-edit;
// change the constants in that script and re-run instead.
//
// Base: 0.23 kV L-L, 0.050000 MVA -> Zbase 1.0580 ohm. Per-branch r/x are carried through exactly
// as resistance_ohm_per_km / reactance_ohm_per_km on each line; the
// line_configuration objects hold the nominal per-conductor values and exist
// for real GridLAB-D tooling (the explicit per-line values take priority in
// this backend's parser). capacity_kw comes from the MATPOWER rateA column.
//
// ref_lv_bus_1 is the MV substation busbar (12.7 kV L-N
// -> ~22 kV L-L on ABCN, matching TRANSFORMER_MV_KV). Its LV network hangs off
// 5 distribution transformers (pcc_1..pcc_5) rather than lines, which is
// what makes each group a microgrid zone: the loader partitions on the line-only
// graph, so everything behind a transformer is one zone, and that transformer is
// the PCC tripped to island it. Zone codes come from the transformer names
// (1..5) -- keep them below IOT_NUM_ZONES (default 10) or the
// parent bridge hashes them to an arbitrary zone_<n> stream instead of routing.
//
//
// Loads are the 2021-01-01 00:00:00 snapshot of p_load/q_load (VA), not a time series.
// No DER: the dataset ships none and this grid has no PV profile, so no
// zone can hold voltage on its own if islanded.
module powerflow;
module generators;

